Two observations:  First, the unique items required to win the game
are sufficiently special that it is worthwhile for a special section
of the savefile near the beginning to record exactly where they are.

As matters stand, if *any* part of the savefile is corrupt on disk, 
you lose the rest of the savefile - and those unique items if they 
happen to be stored after it.  And let's face it, they usually are.
 
Secondly, the savefiles need a major redesign *anyway* for purposes 
of both robustness and cross-platform compatibility. 

There are a bunch of encoding tricks that could be used to make 
savefiles many times more recoverable.  A file header section 
giving byte offsets of anything that follows a variable-length 
section would be a good start.  But going cross-platform with an
ascii-encoded savefile with unique, unambiguous delimiters between 
sections instead of writing binary data would be even better.  Of 
course it would be more robust.  But for another thing it would 
allow people to continue games on machines other than the one on 
which they started.  And for another, it would make the Hearse's 
job ever so much easier.

